# Development Workflow

## Git Commits

- Use Conventional Commits format
- Use Commit Lint for validation
- Use Left Hook for pre-commit hooks

### Conventional Commits Format

```
type(scope): description

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

### Commit Types

- `feat`: New feature
- `fix`: Bug fix
- `docs`: Documentation changes
- `style`: Code style changes (formatting, etc.)
- `refactor`: Code refactoring
- `test`: Adding or updating tests
- `chore`: Maintenance tasks
- `perf`: Performance improvements
- `ci`: CI/CD changes
- `build`: Build system changes

### Examples

```
feat(events): add rich text editor for events
fix(auth): resolve login redirect issue
docs(readme): update installation instructions
style(ui): improve button component styling
refactor(api): simplify event creation endpoint
test(components): add tests for EventCard component
```

## Branch Strategy

- Use feature branches for new features
- Use descriptive branch names
- Implement proper code review process
- Use pull requests for all changes

### Branch Naming

```
feature/event-management
bugfix/auth-redirect
hotfix/critical-error
refactor/api-structure
docs/update-readme
```

## Code Quality

- ESLint for code linting
- Prettier for code formatting
- Stylelint for CSS/SCSS linting
- TypeScript strict mode
- Automated testing

## Environment Setup

- Use proper environment variables
- Implement development/staging/production configs
- Use proper secret management

## Code Review Process

- All changes require pull request
- Minimum 1 reviewer approval
- Automated checks must pass
- Code coverage requirements
- Documentation updates

### Review Checklist

- [ ] Code follows project conventions
- [ ] Tests are included and passing
- [ ] Documentation is updated
- [ ] No security vulnerabilities
- [ ] Performance impact considered
- [ ] Accessibility requirements met

## Testing Strategy

- Unit tests for utilities and hooks
- Component tests for UI components
- Integration tests for features
- E2E tests for critical paths
- Performance testing

### Test Requirements

- Minimum 80% code coverage
- All new features must have tests
- Bug fixes must include regression tests
- Performance tests for critical paths

## Deployment

- Use Vercel for hosting
- Implement proper CI/CD pipeline
- Use environment-specific builds
- Implement rollback strategy

### Deployment Pipeline

1. Code commit to main branch
2. Automated tests run
3. Build process starts
4. Deployment to staging
5. Manual testing on staging
6. Deployment to production
7. Post-deployment verification

## Monitoring and Logging

- Implement error tracking
- Monitor performance metrics
- Track user analytics
- Log important events

### Error Tracking

```typescript
// Error boundary
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  componentDidCatch(error: Error, errorInfo: ErrorInfo) {
    // Log error to monitoring service
    logError(error, errorInfo);
  }
}

// API error handling
const handleApiError = (error: ApiError) => {
  logError('API Error', {
    endpoint: error.config?.url,
    status: error.response?.status,
    message: error.message,
  });
};
```

## Documentation

- **Always use English** for all documentation, README files, API docs, and technical guides
- Keep README up to date
- Document API endpoints with clear examples
- Maintain component documentation with usage examples
- Update deployment guides when processes change
- Write clear, concise, and beginner-friendly documentation
- Include code examples and practical use cases
- Document breaking changes and migration guides
- Place all documentation in /docs directory of root

### Documentation Structure

```
docs/
├── getting-started.md
├── architecture.md
├── api-reference.md
├── deployment.md
├── contributing.md
└── troubleshooting.md
```

